course: everyday
lesson: 96
title: Grandparents, divorce and step-families
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Grandparents carry family history that only they can tell; listening to their stories and asking questions is a gift to both of you, and if a grandparent becomes forgetful because of an illness like dementia, they still love you and need patience.
explain: Different homes have different rules, and kindly following the rules of the house you are in keeps everyone happy.
explain: Divorce is about the grown-ups' relationship, not about the children: it is never the child's fault, both parents still love them, and children should never be put in the middle, carrying messages or choosing sides.
explain: Step-families and blended families take time to work out; being polite and giving each other time and space helps, and if you are adopted or fostered, your story is yours to share or keep.
explain: A boy in Wellington is asked by his dad to tell his mum the weekend plans; he says kindly, «Dad, I think you and Mum should talk about that», and both parents agree he is right.
fact: Grandparents' stories are family history that only they can tell.
fact: Some older people get memory illnesses like dementia, and they still love their family.
fact: Being patient and reminding kindly helps a forgetful grandparent.
fact: Follow the rules of the house you are in, kindly.
fact: Divorce is never the child's fault, and both parents still love the child.
fact: Children should not be asked to choose between parents or put in the middle.
fact: Carrying messages between parents is not a child's job.
fact: Most divorces are final, and accepting this helps a child heal.
fact: Getting close to a new step-parent takes time for everyone, so be polite and patient.
fact: Blended families get on better when they find shared activities and give each other space.
fact: An adopted or fostered child can choose what to share about their story.
exercise: Grandpa tells stories about when he was a boy. What is a good thing to do? | Listen and ask him questions | Look at your phone until he finishes | Tell him those days are boring | Ask him to tell a story from TV instead
exercise: Why are grandparents' stories so valuable? | They are family history only they can tell | They are always more exciting than books | They teach you how to get presents | They are the same as what you learn at school
exercise: Grandma keeps forgetting your name and asks the same question many times. What should you do? | Be patient and gently remind her | Get cross and tell her to remember | Laugh at her for forgetting | Stop visiting until she gets better
exercise: What might cause a grandparent to become very forgetful? | A memory illness such as dementia | Not loving their family any more | Not trying hard enough to remember | Eating too many sweets as a child
exercise: At Grandad's house you must take your shoes off, but at home you do not. What should you do? | Follow Grandad's rule while you are at his house | Keep your shoes on because your parents allow it | Argue that his rule is wrong | Refuse to visit until he changes the rule
exercise: Your parents tell you they are getting divorced. What is true? | It is not your fault, and they both still love you | You caused it by being naughty | One parent will stop loving you | You must choose which parent to live with, and tell them by tomorrow
exercise: Your mum says bad things about your dad. What can you say? | I love you both and I don't want to choose | You're right, Dad is a bad person | I'll go and tell Dad everything you just said about him | I'm going to stop seeing Dad now
exercise: Ruby keeps hoping her divorced parents will get back together. What helps her? | Sharing the feeling, but understanding it is their decision | Making a plan to trick them into meeting | Being naughty so they have to work together | Refusing to speak to anyone until her parents get back together again
exercise: Dad asks Leo to tell Mum that she is late with the money. What should Leo do? | Politely say they should talk to each other | Carry the message exactly as Dad said it | Add his own angry words to the message | Promise to keep the message a secret from Mum
exercise: Why should children not carry messages between divorced parents? | It puts them in the middle of grown-up problems | Children always forget the messages | Parents are not allowed to talk to each other any more after a divorce | It makes the parents get back together
exercise: Mia has a new step-dad and is not sure about him yet. What is best? | Give him time and be polite | Ignore him until he goes away | Tell him he can never be part of the family | Pretend to love him straight away
exercise: Two families join and now Sam has step-sisters. What helps? | Find things they all enjoy and give each other space | Compete to be their parents' favourite by being better at everything | Stay in his room and avoid them | Make rules that only suit himself
exercise: A classmate asks Ella, who is adopted, lots of questions about her birth parents. What is right? | Ella shares only what she wants to share | Ella must answer every single question she is asked | Ella should lie so nobody asks again | Ella should be ashamed of being adopted
test: A girl in Edinburgh feels her parents split up because she argued with her brother so much. What is true? | Divorce is about the grown-ups, never the children | Her arguing caused it, so she must stop | If she is good from now on, they will surely get back together | Her brother caused it, not her
test: At your aunt's house, dinner is eaten at the table with no screens, but at home you eat watching TV. What is the kind thing to do there? | Eat at the table with no screen, as your aunt asks | Bring the tablet to the table, because it is allowed at home | Eat in the other room so you can watch | Tell your aunt that her rule is old-fashioned
test: A boy's grandad, who has dementia, calls him by his dad's name. What is the most loving response? | Smile and gently say his own name | Tell him he is wrong and getting worse | Walk away and refuse to talk to him | Decide that his grandad no longer loves him
test: A father asks his son, «Who do you love more, me or your mum?» What is the best answer? | I love you both and do not want to choose | I love you more because you asked | I love Mum more but I will not tell her | I do not love either of you any more, so stop asking
test: Which is the wisest thought for a child whose parents have just divorced? | It will take time, but both my parents still love me | If I am perfect from now on, they will surely get back together | One of them must not want me any more | It is my job to keep them both happy
test: Why does it take time to feel close to a new step-parent? | Getting to know someone well always takes time | Step-parents are not allowed to be kind | It means the step-parent is a bad person | Children should never get close to step-parents
test: A boy is in foster care. A new friend asks why he does not live with his parents. What can he do? | Say as much or as little as he wants | He must tell the full story to everyone | He has to make up a different story | He must ask a teacher to answer for him
test: A grandmother tells the story of moving to Canada as a girl. Why should her grandchildren ask questions? | Her memories are family history that only she can tell | It is the only way to pass a history test | Older people only like children who ask them lots of questions | So she will tell the story less often
test: Which shows a child is being put in the middle of a divorce? | Being asked to carry messages or take sides | Spending weekends at each parent's home | Having a bedroom in two different houses | Being told by both parents that they love you
test: grandad asked me same q 3 times today, so annoying | Be patient and remind him kindly | Tell him to stop asking the same thing | Ignore him until he remembers | Laugh at him for forgetting
test: mum n dad splitting up, is it cos i was bad | No, divorce is never the child's fault | Yes, children cause divorce | Yes, if you were naughty a lot of the time | Maybe, so try to be perfect
test: dad wants me to tell mum shes late with the money again | Carrying messages between parents is not your job | Yes, you must pass it on | Yes, and add what you think about it too | Yes, but write it down for her
test: new stepdad's ok i guess but feels weird | It takes time, be polite and patient | Ignore him until he leaves | Tell him straight out he is not your dad | Pretend he is not there
explain: A boy in Ohio believed his parents' divorce was his fault and kept it secret for a year; he became quiet and angry until a counsellor helped him see it was not his fault at all.
explain: A girl in Sydney did not want to visit her grandma in hospital, and later wished she had; she wrote her a letter instead and her grandma kept it by the bed.
fact: Children often wrongly believe their parents' divorce is their fault; it never is.
fact: When a parent marries again (remarries), the new husband or wife becomes the child's step-parent, so a child can have a dad and a stepdad.
fact: Some unhappy couples say they stay together «for the kids», meaning for the children's sake.
fact: Bottling up strong feelings about big family changes can make children withdrawn, angry or unwell.
test: parents splitting up, pretty sure it's cos of me | It's never a child's fault, talk to a trusted adult | Yes, it's your fault, so try to be perfect to fix it | Yes, you should run away | Keep quiet so it doesn't get worse
test: new step-dad, i'm so angry, not gonna talk to anyone | It's ok to feel angry, tell a trusted adult how you feel | Shout at him every day so he knows how angry you are | Bottle it up, it goes away | Break his things
test: nan's poorly and forgets my name, it hurts | Tell a parent how it feels, it's an illness not about you | It means she doesn't love you any more, so stop going | Stop visiting her | Shout so she remembers
test: heard Lily's mum n dad are splitting up cos she failed her spelling test, her mate told me | Do not spread it; divorce is never the child's fault, so that part is surely untrue | It is true, bad marks can cause a divorce | It is true, so Lily should study harder now so that her parents stay together | Pass it on to everyone so that Lily knows how many people understand her
test: my cousin says step-mums are always evil, like in the films, n mine's gonna be the same | Films are made up; getting close to a step-parent takes time, so be polite and patient | It is true, every step-mum is wicked like in the films, so keep away from her | It is true, so be rude to her first | It is true unless she buys you presents
test: the old man at the corner shop forgot his own grandson's name n the kids are saying he's gone mad | Do not laugh or spread it; memory illnesses like dementia are illnesses, and he still loves his family | He has gone mad, so the kids are right to say it and laugh at him about it | He must not love his grandson any more, otherwise he would remember his name easily | It is funny, so everyone should hear about it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the cause of a divorce between parents? | A divorce is never the fault of the child, and both parents continue to love the child | A divorce is generally caused by the child's poor behaviour or poor marks at school | A divorce indicates that at least one parent has ceased to love the child altogether | A divorce may be reversed if the child behaves exceptionally well thereafter
exam: Identify the response consistent with the established facts concerning the conveyance of messages between separated parents. | Carrying messages between parents is not the child's job | Carrying messages between parents is the child's duty | Carrying messages is required by law of every child | Carrying messages should always be done in secret by the child
exam: Pursuant to a grandparent suffering from a memory illness such as dementia, select the appropriate conduct. | Patience and kind reminders, since the grandparent still loves the family | Correcting every error loudly, so that the grandparent will learn faster | Avoiding all visits, since the grandparent no longer recognises affection | Laughing at the errors, since the grandparent will not remember them
exam: In respect of an adopted or fostered child, which statement is correct? | The child may choose what to share about their own story | The child is obliged to tell the whole class their story | The child must never mention adoption or fostering at all | The child's classmates are entitled to know every detail
test: overheard nan: "between you n me, he's moved in with her already" bout my dad | Dad may now live with a new partner; that is grown-up talk, so ask mum or dad gently how things are | It means dad is just visiting a friend for a few nights and nothing has changed at home | It means you must shout at dad | It means you must keep it secret forever and never ask anyone in the family about it
test: kids saying Ella's "got two dads now" cos her mum remarried, n laughing | Ella has a dad and a stepdad; that is a normal family, so do not laugh and stand by Ella | It means Ella's dad is a twin, so now there are two of him living in her house | It is funny, so laugh too | It means Ella's family is bad and broken, so kids should stay away from her
test: auntie said "they're only staying together for the kids" bout my cousin's parents | She thinks they are unhappy as a couple but stay together for the children; it is private, so do not repeat it | It means they love their kids too little to bother splitting up, so the kids are a nuisance to them | It means the kids must leave | Tell your cousin straight away what auntie said, because he has a right to know everything
